The obj and bin folders contain compiled binaries and temporary files that should not be tracked in Git. If they're showing up in your Git changes, follow these steps to ignore them properly:

1. Add to .gitignore

Make sure your repository has a .gitignore file. If it doesn't, create one in the root of your project and add the following lines:

# Ignore build output
bin/
obj/

2. Remove Already Tracked Files

If Git is already tracking these files, simply adding them to .gitignore won't remove them. You need to untrack them with:

git rm -r --cached bin obj
git commit -m "Removed bin and obj from tracking"
git push origin <your-branch>

3. Verify

Run git status again. The bin and obj folders should no longer appear in the changes.

